<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hindu Kush</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> Kush is a mountain range in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> that separates <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-subcontinent">Indian subcontinent</a>. Its passes were the routes used by invaders entering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> from the north-west. Rulers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chagatai-khanate">Chagatai Khanate</a>, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duwa">Duwa</a>, raided across it into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a>'s territory.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hindu-kush.jpg" alt="Hindu Kush" width="320" height="178" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ninara from Helsinki, Finland · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hindu_Kush_mountains_(30357238428).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
